# Story 1.12 review P11 / D3: enforce LF line endings on committed perf
# baseline JSON files. `serde_json::to_string_pretty` emits `\n`; without this
# rule, Windows checkouts with `core.autocrlf=true` convert to `\r\n` and the
# next test run re-writes with `\n` — every Windows run dirties the file.
# Scoping kept narrow on purpose (no global `* text=auto`) to avoid changing
# behavior for the rest of the repo until a dedicated repo-hygiene story
# tackles end-of-line policy holistically.
tests/perf-baselines/*.json text eol=lf

# Story 2.4 (FR-2): round-trip fixture corpus files are byte-exact test
# inputs — the `round_trip_subset` gate asserts serialize(analyze(f)) == f
# byte-for-byte, and some fixtures deliberately carry trailing whitespace or
# no trailing newline. `-text` opts them out of ALL EOL conversion so a
# Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true` cannot silently rewrite fixture
# bytes and turn the gate into a liar. Scoping kept narrow on purpose
# (mirrors the perf-baselines rule above).
crates/orgsidian-parser/tests/fixtures/round_trip/*.org -text

# Story 2.8: the `orgsidian parse` CLI fixture asserts *semantic* output
# (SCHEDULED stamp, :PROPERTIES: → :ID:), not EOL handling. On a Windows
# checkout with `core.autocrlf=true` it becomes CRLF, and the vendored
# grammar then emits no `plan` / `property_drawer` nodes — so `scheduled`
# and `properties` come back empty and the two parse_cmd tests fail. That
# grammar-level CRLF gap is a real cross-platform bug tracked separately;
# `-text` here only keeps this fixture's bytes identical on every platform
# so the CLI test asserts what it means to assert. Same narrow scoping as
# the rules above — this is NOT a repo-wide EOL policy.
crates/orgsidian-cli/tests/fixtures/*.org -text

# Story 2.5 (LD-44): generated corpus manifests are regular git files (NOT
# LFS — the Story 2.6 per-PR gate must work on an LFS-free checkout; embedded
# content is JSON-escaped so EOL-sensitive bytes live inside string literals).
# The JSON files' own line endings stay LF like perf-baselines above.
fixtures/*.json text eol=lf

# Story 2.5 (LD-44): the materialized vault corpus goes through git-LFS —
# ~2k+ generated .org files consumed only by nightly/L2 jobs and tooling
# (the per-PR subset is embedded in fixtures/subset-pr.json instead).
# `-text` rides along: corpus bytes are EOL-sensitive by design (CRLF/mixed
# edge bucket). Markdown (README.md provenance note) stays regular git —
# the pattern matches *.org only.
# FOLLOWUP(LFS-migration): Story 2.6 checked (2026-06-11) — git-lfs still
# unavailable on this machine; corpus stays as raw git objects. Re-deferred:
# owner is the first maintainer machine WITH git-lfs, after the Epic-2 story
# stack (2.3→2.6) merges. CAVEAT — do NOT run `git lfs migrate import`
# while stacked branches/PRs are open: it rewrites every commit touching the
# corpus and orphans them. Non-rewriting alternative for the future owner:
# uncomment the stanza below + `git lfs install` + `git add --renormalize
# tests/fixtures/vault-corpus` in a normal commit (old raw blobs stay in
# history, ~2.3 MB, acceptable; future versions go through LFS).
# tests/fixtures/vault-corpus/**/*.org filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
tests/fixtures/vault-corpus/**/*.org -text
